                                     C-INDEX/II
                                PRODUCT INFORMATION


            C-Index/II is the advanced C-Index product. Several of the
            advanced features that C-Index/II are:

                 Files up to 8.7 Terabytes in size.
                 Segmented (concatenated) keys.
                 Binary, numeric and custom keys.
                 Easy porting to new systems.
                 File compatibility across diverse hardware.
                 Windows memory management.


            DIFFERENCES BETWEEN C-INDEX/II AND C-INDEX/PC

            The following information lists the differences between
            C-Index/II and C-Index/PC:


            1. Increased Maximum File Size            ______________________________

            C-Index/II uses 32 bit node pointers resulting in a maximum
            file size of 8.7 Terabytes (trillion bytes).  C-Index/PC has
            a maximum file size of 32 Megabytes.


            2. Non-String Keys            __________________

            C-Index/II supports index key values of any type, strings,
            numerics, binary and custom.  The default value for indexes
            is the string type.  No special steps need be taken to
            specify string type keys.  To use any other type of key the
            application program indicates to C-Index the index key type,
            either when creating the file or by using the cchgndx()
            function.

            C-Index/PC supports null terminated string keys only.


            3. Segmented Keys            _________________

            C-Index/II provides for specifying segmented keys in single-
            key and multi-key routines.  Segmented keys (also called
            concatenated keys in some data managers) allows an
            application to create a key that is made up of several
            fields.  C-Index/II allows the keys to be of differing
            lengths and types.  Segmented keys are variable in length.
            There are no padding bytes added to the key to make the
            individual segments position to fixed locations.  Instead,
            C-Index/II keeps track of each segment separately and stores
            only the number of bytes needed for that value.



                                                                        6


            When using the single-key routines, the application must
            create the correct key format for all index operations.  The
            format is a one byte (unsigned char) length indicator
            followed by each key segment value stored end-to-end.

            Multi-key routines automatically build the correct segmented
            key values based on the datalist and indexlist
            specification.  The dsetkey() function can also be used to
            create search keys for dfind().


            4. File Create Functions            ________________________

            C-Index/II has two functions, bcreate() and dbcreate(), to
            provide control over file mode and index type.


            5. File Open Functions and Read-Only File Open Type            ___________________________________________________

            C-Index/II has two function calls, bopen() and dbopen(), for
            opening the index file.  These provide control for the
            readonly mode and specifying index types (other than
            string).  The additional read mode parameter allows the
            specification of read-only access for the opened file.  This
            solves problems for some LAN systems where users need to be
            restricted to read only access for specific files.  The
            bopen() and dbopen() functions are used in place of the
            mopen() and dmopen() functions of C-Index/PC.  An indexlist
            parameter specifies files with non-string indexes.

            6. Custom Keys            ______________

            C-Index/II has five index key types reserved for
            customization.  This allows the user to implement any type
            of custom key value and collating sequence.  The function of
            the custom key values are defined in the compiler specific
            source files.  The format of the custom key entry must begin
            with a one byte length count.  Otherwise, the action of the
            custom key comparison is entirely under the control of the
            code defined by the user.



                                                                        7


            7. Multi-Key Functions Support New Key Types            ____________________________________________

            The C-Index/II Multi-Key functions allow you to specify any
            field as a key.  The keytype field of the datalist supports
            the following types:

            Field     Key
            Type      Type            ______________
            Any       NONKEY         Not a keyed field.
            STRINGFLD STRINGIND      ASCIIZ string keys.
            STRINGFLD NOCASEIND      ASCIIZ string key, ignore case
            BINARYFLD BINARYIND      Binary keys.
            SHORTFLD  SHORTIND       Short integer keys (16 bit).
            INTFLD    INTIND         Integer keys (compiler specific).
            LONGFLD   LONGIND        Long integer keys (32 bit).
            FLOATFLD  FLOATIND       Float keys (compiler specific).
            DOUBLEFLD DOUBLEIND      Double keys (compiler specific).
            SEGFLD    SEGIND         Segmented keys.
            BINARYFLD CUS1IND        Custom key #1 (customer supplied).
            BINARYFLD CUS2IND        Custom key #2 (customer supplied).
            BINARYFLD CUS3IND        Custom key #3 (customer supplied).
            BINARYFLD CUS4IND        Custom key #4 (customer supplied).
            BINARYFLD CUS5IND        Custom key #5 (customer supplied).


            8. Creating the Search Key using dsetkey            ________________________________________

            C-Index has a function, "dsetkey", to simplify creating a
            key value for use with dfind.  The dsetkey function returns
            the correct key value based on the index number and a
            datalist.  The datalist would typically be the same as the
            one used for the dread() function.  Before calling dsetkey
            the field values required for the key must be stored in the
            field locations pointed to by the datalist.  The dsetkey
            function then reads these values and builds a key.  For most
            types of keys it is not useful to call this function,
            however, for binary and segmented keys, it simplifies the
            process so that you do not need to be concerned with
            internal representations of the search key.

            9. Diverse Computer File Compatibility            ______________________________________

            C-Index/II has provisions for reading and writing files on a
            machine with an architecture different than Intel.  This
            involves changing the byte order of shorts, ints, and longs
            as required by the specific machine reading the file.  For
            example, 68000 computers and 370 mainframes use the high to
            low value byte ordering, whereas 8086 family processors and
            VAX computers use low to high value byte ordering.  With
            this feature data files can be created using any computer
            and read on any computer.



                                                                        8


            10. Full Tree Deletion            ______________________

            C-Index/II has the ability to re-use nodes that have been
            made empty through deletion of keys.  For example, this
            allows the program to add any combination of keys, delete
            all of the keys, and then re-add any other combination of
            keys without any wasted space being left in the file.
            C-Index/PC has partial space reclamation that reuses the
            space under most circumstances.


            11. Improved Reading Performance in Shared Mode            _______________________________________________

            When a file is opened in shared mode in C-Index/PC, the
            cnext, cprev, and dseq functions must always simulate
            sequential access by repositioning the current pointer with
            the cfind function.  C-Index/II improves this by requiring
            repositioning only in the event that the file has been
            changed.


            12. C-Index/II File Format            __________________________

            C-Index/II and C-Index/PC file formats are not the same.
            The larger maximum file size of C-Index/II requires the file
            header and node header information to be stored as 32 bits.

            The C-index/II header includes space for "custom"
            information, a 100 byte binary record that can be written
            and read directly from the header.  Also, the header has a
            field for the number of records stored in the file and the
            number of deleted record numbers stored in DELINDX (used
            internally by the multi-key routines).

            A general purpose utility program, convert, is provided to
            read the file in C-Index/PC format and write it out in
            C-Index/II format.  A new function, cconvert has been added
            for converting files from within an application.  This
            functions works in a similar manner to the cbuild function.


            13. Source Code and Portability            _______________________________

            C-Index/II source code uses a more conventional method for
            building and maintaining the libraries.  The C-Index/II code
            is easier than C-Index/PC to port to new compilers and
            operating systems.  C-Index/PC only supports 16 bit PC type
            environments.  C-Index/II can be ported to any environment.


            14. Changeable Node Size            ________________________

            With changes to the source code, the C-Index/II node size
            may be changed to any arbitrary size that is at least 1K.



                                                                        9


            The typical application of this feature is in CDROM access
            where having larger nodes improves access time on slow
            optical disk media.  C-Index/PC only support 1K node sizes.


            15. Enhanced C-Index/II Memory Allocation            _________________________________________

            In C-Index/II the space for storing current key information
            is allocated only when an index is used by the program, and
            released when the file is closed.  The new allocation code
            is designed to work correctly with Windows and Mac memory
            allocation requirements via operating system specific
            interface code that can easily be modified to meet custom
            methods of memory allocation.  In addition, the virtual disk
            buffers are allocated from heap space.  Compatibility is
            provided for mixed model memory where the heap is in far
            space.  The code will also work where the buffers have been
            allocated from movable heap space, such as with Windows and
            Macintosh systems.

            C-Index/PC memory is entirely statically allocated at
            compile time.


            16. Faster Addition of Pre-Sorted Keys            ______________________________________

            C-Index/II uses fewer key comparisons for insertion by
            employing a modified binary search of the index nodes
            instead of a linear search.  This new technique results in
            approximately 6 times speed improvement for sequential key
            insertion and also improves performance for non-sorted key
            insertion and key retrieval.



                                                                        10


            17. Record Counting Primitives            ______________________________

            C-Index/II has two single-key routines to assist in keeping
            track of the number of records that are stored in the file.
            These primitives are used by the Multi-Key routines to
            maintain the number of active records in a file.

            C-Index/PC does not count records contained in the file.


            18. Multi-Key Record Count            __________________________

            The C-Index/II Multi-Key routines automatically keep track
            of the number of multi-key records stored in the file.  The
            multi-key function, dgetcnt() returns the active record
            count in the psp->rec variable.


            19. Custom Header Information            _____________________________

            Two C-Index/II functions allow the application to read and
            write a binary block of information into the header.  The
            maximum number of bytes that may be stored in the header is
            defined by MAXCUSTINFO (default value of 100).  C-Index/PC
            does not have custom header information.


            20. Turning Off Multi-Key Record Number Reassignment            ____________________________________________________

            C-Index/PC automatically reuses record numbers.  C-Index/II
            allows the application program to specify whether or not to
            reuse record numbers.



                                                                        11


            21. Improvements to the Dump Utility            ____________________________________

            The C-Index/II Dump utility is enhanced with five features
            not found in C-Index/PC.

            First, at the command line there is an option, seq, which
            will cause the leaf nodes to be printed sequentially.

            The second feature is the ability to print individual nodes
            while in the manual mode.  This is convenient for searching
            through an index and then printing out the specific node of
            interest.

            The third additional feature is the printing of key values
            in hex and ASCII.  This allows for viewing of non-string key
            values.

            The fourth feature is to display the new header fields for
            custom information, delete and record counts, and index key
            types.

            The fifth feauture is the ability to view a file that is
            being modified by a C-Index program that is running as a
            separate process.  Commands allow for locking and unlocking
            the file to be able to view the file in a non-changing
            state, if desired.



                                                                        12


            CHANGES TO EXISTING PROGRAMS REQUIRED FOR C-INDEX/II

            C-Index/II is almost completely program compatible with
            C-Index/PC.  A new file structure is used that requires
            converting existing C-Index/PC files to the C-Index/II
            format using the supplied utility programs and function
            calls.  In order to convert existing C-Index/PC programs for
            C-Index/II the following changes are required:

            1. New Header File            __________________

            In place of CINDEX.H and CINDEXSB.H the application must
            include CNDX.H.  This in turn includes a compiler specific
            header file, such as CNDXTC.H in the case of Borland C.
            Both CNDX.H and the compiler specific header file must be 
            accessible to the compiler by moving them to the application 
            directory or adding an include file directory command to the 
            application module.


            2. Compiler Define            __________________

            The compile command line for the application must have a
            define for the type of compiler and operating system being
            used so that the correct compiler specific header file will
            be included.  For example, the compile line for the example
            program (compiled using Borland C++ to run under MSDOS)
            contains the defines:

                 -DTC -DDOS

            to specify the Borland (or Turbo) C compiler and the MSDOS 
            operating system.


            3. -Zp Flag is not used
            _______________________________________

            C-Index/PC require the use of the -Zp option when compiling
            with some C compilers.  The make file and pre-compiled libraries
            supplied with the release version of C-Index/II do not use
            the -Zp flag.  You can recompile the library with or without
            the -Zp flag and the structure of the C-Index/II header file
            will not be affected.  If you have previously modified
            C-Index/PC libraries to not use -Zp, you will have files
            that are incompatible with the conversion utility and
            conversion function call.  This will require that you run
            the hdrconv utility before running the convert utility to
            update the header information to the standard format.



                                                                        13


            4. Number of Buffers            ____________________

            The number of buffers is not changeable in the C-Index/II
            header file.  Instead, if you need to modify the number of
            buffers, the global variable bcbnum must be set in the
            application program to the number of desired buffers before
            any C-Index files are opened.


            5. Allocated Memory            ___________________

            Unlike C-Index/PC, C-Index/II uses memory allocation to
            store information about the current index pointer location.
            This saves considerable space in the parameter structure but
            increases the space required in the heap.  For applications
            that are short on memory, this may require changes to ensure
            that the heap will be large enough.  Running out of heap
            space during any C-Index operation will result in the return
            of the OUTOFMEM error.

            If the parameter structure has been allocated off of the
            stack as an automatic variable, the stack may not need to be
            as large as before.  However, it is advisable to be sure the
            application stack is large enough, since stack bugs are
            difficult to detect and fix.


            6. Semaphore Keys            _________________

            If your application uses the msetsema, mtstsema, or mclrsema
            functions, you will need to change your functions to create
            C-Index/II compatible binary semaphore keys, instead of
            C-Index/PC string semaphore keys.

            8. Record Locking            _________________

            Some previous versions of C-Index/PC have not required that
            a multi-key record be locked before preforming dupdate or
            ddelete.  C-Index/II does require such a lock when the file
            is open in SHARED mode.  If your application does not lock
            records in SHARED mode, either add record locking calls or
            turn off this locking requirement by setting the nolock
            parameter structure variable:

                 psp->nolock = TRUE;






                        C-INDEX Current Versions and Prices

            C-Index/II
            Works with All Computer Systems
            $695.00
            Release 4.1

            C-Index/II is a functional superset of C-Index/PC.  Major
            feature enhancements include:

                 Files up to 8.7 Terabytes in size.
                 Segmented (concatenated) keys.
                 Binary, numeric and custom keys.
                 Easier porting to new systems.
                 File compatibility across diverse hardware.
                 Improved Windows memory management.
                 Increased maximum record size (30K).




            C-Index/PC
            Works with MSDOS
            $195.00
            Release 3.4

            C-Index/PC has all the major features of C-Index/II and is
            designed to work in the IBM PC environment.  Complete source
            code is included for the Borland C/C++ under MSDOS.  C-Index/PC 
            is portable to all 16 bit IBM PC operating systems, including 
            Microsoft Windows (technical support is only provided for MSDOS).

            Registered users of C-Index/PC may upgrade to C-Index/II for an  
            additional $500 (the difference in price).
